00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:44.050
So without further
ado, let's get

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:45.920
to answering your questions.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.920
The first question
is about kratom.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.070
And the question is, what are
the short and long-term effects

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.450
of taking kratom?

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.430
For those of you that
haven't heard of kratom,

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:57.190
kratom is a substance that
comes from a tree that

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.050
grows naturally in Indonesia.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:02.680
The tree itself is called
mitragyna speciosa.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.780
And it's been known for
hundreds of years or more.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.009
We don't really know how long,
but at least for a couple

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:11.500
of hundred years,
that when people chew

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:14.920
the leaves of this tree, they
experience a mild stimulant

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:15.520
effect.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.440
And there's also a
process of extraction

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.740
whereby you can take the leaves
and get high concentrations

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.320
of kratom that
nowadays is packaged

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.460
into various supplements.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.590
Most often consumed
in capsule form,

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.710
although sometimes it's
sold in raw powder form.

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:32.050
Here's the key thing to
understand about kratom.

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:36.080
Indeed at low dosages, kratom
has a mild stimulant effect.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.650
However, at higher
dosages, it has

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.630
what's called an
analgesic effect, that

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:44.830
is a painkilling effect,
and it acts as a sedative.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:48.920
And kratom itself acts
as an opioid in the body.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.430
This is what's making kratom
a very controversial topic

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:52.360
these days.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.940
In particular,
because most people

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:56.770
have heard of the
so-called opioid crisis.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.860
The opioid crisis
has been a term

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.488
coined to largely
center around issues

00:03:01.488 --> 00:03:03.280
that have taken place
in the United States,

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.560
although these issues
definitely extend

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.240
beyond the borders
of the United States.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.610
But the opioid
crisis is essentially

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:12.700
the overconsumption and
widespread addiction

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.300
to opioids.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.720
Opioids include
things like morphine,

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.480
oxycodone, also
called OxyContin,

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:22.360
and there are other opioids
similar to those compounds,

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.540
all of which have the general
effect of being painkillers

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:26.590
and sedatives.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:29.860
And in some people,
again some people,

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.710
eliciting a sense of
euphoria in particular

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:34.390
at low to moderate dosages.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:37.840
However, people quickly develop
a tolerance to those drugs

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.060
and need to consume
more and more of them

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:42.280
in order to get the
effect that they initially

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:43.720
got from a lower dosage.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.240
And they are not just
highly habit-forming.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.560
They are highly addictive.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.330
And we can distinguish
between habit

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:53.110
forming and addictive by simply
saying that addictive means

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:56.350
people will continue to take
something or do something

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:58.388
despite negative consequences.

00:03:58.388 --> 00:04:00.430
There are other relevant
definitions of addiction

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:01.000
as well.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.828
I define addiction more broadly
as the progressive narrowing

00:04:04.828 --> 00:04:06.370
of the things that
give you pleasure.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:08.410
And indeed, when people
get addicted to opioids,

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.330
it is a very bad picture.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:13.330
It often hampers many,
many areas of their lives,

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:14.920
and seriously so.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:18.339
So the opioid crisis refers
to the overprescription

00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:19.870
of opioid drugs.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:22.029
That's sort of what it
generally taken to mean.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:25.660
But it also includes accessing
opioids such as morphine,

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:29.230
oxycodone, et cetera through
gray market sources through,

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.750
black market sources,
and on and on.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.150
And of course, the
acquisition of the drugs

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:36.340
is just one piece it's
also the over consumption

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:39.470
of those drugs and, of course,
the addiction to those drugs,

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:41.560
which has proved to
be so pernicious.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.420
So what does this have
to do with kratom?

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.610
Kratom is also an opioid.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:47.500
And we'll talk
about how it differs

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.170
in its opioid properties from
the drugs I just talked about

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:54.100
because it is different from
morphine and hydrocodone,

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.860
but it also has some
similar properties as well.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:00.130
But the deal with kratom
is that a good number

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.400
of people out there have
managed to wean themselves

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.460
off opioids such as
morphine and hydrocodone

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:08.540
through the use of kratom.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:11.090
And that has been
used as justification

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:13.610
for keeping kratom on the
market and keeping it legal.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:16.820
However, and this is a
very important however,

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:20.990
kratom itself has also proved
to be not only habit forming

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:25.190
but addictive, especially when
taken at dosages that exceed

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:27.830
that lower dose that
just generally creates

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.440
a bit of stimulant, a little
bit of euphoric effect.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:33.140
When people start taking
higher dosages of kratom,

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.930
it is very clear that it
does become addictive,

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:38.630
and itself is very
hard to come off of,

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:41.090
so much so that people
experience so-called kratom

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:41.850
withdrawal.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.070
Now the reason I
mentioned that kratom

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:44.750
has been used by a
good number of people

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:48.020
to wean themselves off
of the more potent forms

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:50.900
of prescription
opioids is that when

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:54.500
I solicited for questions
about kratom on social media,

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:56.730
it was a very binary response.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.540
In fact, there was one
camp a very rabid camp

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.250
that said kratom is terrible.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:02.090
I took this stuff.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:02.780
I got addicted.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.300
It was extremely
hard to come off of.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.770
There were additional
comments in there,

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.600
such as I know
somebody who literally

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.640
had to go to rehab
because of kratom who

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.340
developed a bunch of other
addictions because of kratom.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:18.270
Essentially many, many responses
of people who had only bad

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:19.620
experiences with kratom.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.950
Meaning they liked
it enough at first

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:25.950
that they continued to take
it, and it became addictive

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:27.790
for them or somebody
that they know.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:30.280
However, there was another
camp that was equally vocal,

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:33.420
which kept saying no if one
really adheres to the lower

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:35.190
dosages of kratom,
kratom itself can

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:37.920
be a useful tool for
getting off other opioids.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.290
And there were even a few bona
fide medical professionals,

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:44.100
medical doctors that is,
and I happen to know them

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:47.790
and their reputations as quite
good, who chimed in and sort of

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:50.190
reconciled the two
camps by saying

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:54.600
indeed, if one can avoid taking
kratom at all, you should.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:57.120
There is no reason to take
this thing thinking that it's

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:58.350
quote-unquote good for you.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:00.360
You would be wise
to avoid taking it

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.660
because there is a high
probability if you take it

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:07.890
at a given dosage or at a dosage
that is going to get your brain

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:11.670
and body into a state
of euphoria, analgesia,

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:14.730
and the sort of
classic opioid effect

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:18.100
that it's going to become habit
forming or addicting for you.

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:20.280
That said, these same
medical professionals

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.070
acknowledged that a fair
number of people that they knew

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:26.610
managed to get off of the
more potent forms of opioids

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:29.987
such as morphine, oxycodone,
and so forth using kratom.

00:07:29.987 --> 00:07:32.070
And they said, well if
it's a choice between being

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:34.500
addicted to morphine and
hydrocodone versus taking

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.650
kratom and addicted to those
substances and kratom somehow

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:40.260
allowed them to taper
off of those substances,

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:43.410
that they would look more
favorably upon kratom

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:47.988
if and only if they would
also commit to progressively

00:07:47.988 --> 00:07:50.280
lowering their dose of kratom
and eventually coming off

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:51.150
of kratom.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:54.780
So the general takeaway from
all of that is if you can avoid

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.090
taking kratom, meaning if
you haven't taken it already,

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:57.630
don't.

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:00.300
And that reminds me of a
very important point, which

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.430
a guest on the Huberman
lab podcast Dr. Robert

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.980
Malenka who's an
M.D. and Ph.D. He's

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:06.870
my colleague at Stanford
School of Medicine.

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:11.610
He's a world expert on the
mechanisms underlying addiction

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:13.890
and dopamine and
drug reinforcement,

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:15.480
among many other things.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:18.370
What he said on the
podcast is absolutely true,

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:21.750
which is that it is impossible
to get addicted to a substance

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:23.370
that you've never consumed.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.750
That might seem obvious but
think about that one again.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:28.380
It is impossible to get
addicted to a substance

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:29.730
that you've never consumed.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:31.410
So I think the safest
statement to make

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.659
is if you have not
tried kratom, you

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:35.730
would be wise to
avoid it because you

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:38.100
stand a chance to
become addicted to it.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:39.690
If, however, you
are somebody that's

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:41.460
addicted to other
forms of opioids,

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.039
you absolutely should talk
to your physician about that

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:45.810
and how to get off
of those opioids.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:48.630
But there is evidence that
some people have used kratom

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:51.450
to successfully wean themselves
off the more potent forms

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:53.760
of opioids that I
talked about, namely

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:57.070
morphine and hydrocodone,
as well as a few others.

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:00.210
Now if you're somebody
who already takes kratom,

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:03.240
you need to be very thoughtful
about the dosage that you take.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.310
And you also need to be
thoughtful about the fact

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:09.220
that people differ dramatically
in their response to opioids.

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.700
This is oh so important,
and people do not

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:12.960
talk about this enough.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.840
We hear, for instance, that, oh,
if people are taking anywhere

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:19.080
from one to five grams, maybe
one to six grams of kratom

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:21.450
per day, that's keeping
it in the dosage

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:24.180
range for which people don't
generally tend to get addicted,

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:25.420
you'll hear things like that.

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:27.390
And by the way, when I
say one to five or one

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:29.640
to six grams per
day, I'm not talking

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:32.452
about the individual compounds
that are present in kratom.

00:09:32.452 --> 00:09:34.410
There are a couple of
different plant alkaloids

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:36.702
I'll talk about in a moment
that are present in kratom.

00:09:36.702 --> 00:09:39.210
And these have different
effects on the opioid system.

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:41.640
So nowadays, some of the
companies that sell kratom.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:43.230
And by the way, this is
sold over the counter

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:43.897
as a supplement.

00:09:43.897 --> 00:09:45.447
It does not require
a prescription

00:09:45.447 --> 00:09:47.280
to purchase, at least
at this point in time,

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:48.240
in the United States.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:52.080
Some of these products will have
a higher concentration of one

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.180
or the other
alkaloids within them

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:55.920
such that you can't
really compare

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:01.320
one gram of one brand of kratom
to one gram of another brand

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:04.290
of kratom because they can
have wildly different levels

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:05.550
of these different alkaloids.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:08.720
And these different alkaloids
have wildly different impact

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:12.530
on different aspects of
your brain and body biology.

00:10:12.530 --> 00:10:15.050
In particular, how much
they tend to impact

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:17.930
the so-called endogenous
opioid system, OK.

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:19.400
So when we talk
about dosages, we

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.150
have to keep this in
the back of our mind.

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:23.060
And we have to keep in
the back of our mind

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:26.390
that there does seem to
be a subgroup of people.

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:28.680
We don't know how big this
subgroup of people is,

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:32.360
but we know it's not everyone,
but it's somewhere between 10

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:36.410
and 40% of people seem
to respond to opioids

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:38.030
in a particularly potent way.

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:39.740
They really like them.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:43.850
And perhaps not surprisingly,
people outside of that category

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:45.320
don't tend to like opioids.

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:47.360
I can certainly say
that I am somebody

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:50.390
who, when I've been
prescribed things like Vicodin

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:53.900
or any other opioid
post-surgery for pain,

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:55.820
I hate taking those drugs.

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:57.020
I absolutely hate it.

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:58.460
I'd rather deal with the pain.

00:10:58.460 --> 00:10:59.690
They make me nauseous.

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:01.700
They make me feel terrible.

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:05.510
That said, I know
people who love opioids.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:09.330
It's like a natural fit for
their chemistry in the sense

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:11.280
that it tends to
evoke more euphoria.

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:13.207
They just immediately
like the feeling.

00:11:13.207 --> 00:11:15.540
Some of you may remember the
episode of the Huberman Lab

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:17.610
podcast that I
did about alcohol.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:21.150
And there, too, I discussed
the fact that about 8% to 10%

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:25.050
of people who consume alcohol
get an increased dopamine

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:28.080
response to alcohol that's
not observed in other people.

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:32.970
And that particular subgroup
of people is very, very at risk

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:34.590
for developing
alcohol use disorder,

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:38.880
sometimes called alcoholism,
because they can drink in a way

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:42.240
and they experience a high
from drinking in a way

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:45.013
that other people simply
do not experience.

00:11:45.013 --> 00:11:47.430
Now like everybody else, if
they consume too much alcohol,

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:48.330
they get drunk.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:50.310
So it's not about
the drunk effect,

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:52.663
it's about the dopamine
and other sorts

00:11:52.663 --> 00:11:55.080
of chemicals that are released
in those people in response

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:58.530
to alcohol that other people
just don't seem to experience

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.940
at the same level of potency.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:04.028
So again, with things like
kratom and the other opioids,

00:12:04.028 --> 00:12:06.570
there's a category of people,
and it's a much larger category

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:08.200
of people, again 10% to 40%.

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:10.617
We don't know exactly how many
because the studies are not

00:12:10.617 --> 00:12:13.033
really completed, and there
aren't enough of those studies

00:12:13.033 --> 00:12:14.010
yet, unfortunately.

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.440
But when those people
take an opioid,

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:19.360
wow, oh, wow, do they like it.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:22.500
And those people, in particular,
are very much at risk

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:25.320
for developing an
addiction to opioids.

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:29.040
And so those people, especially,
should never ever go near

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:30.240
kratom if they haven't.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:32.473
Or if they are
already taking kratom,

00:12:32.473 --> 00:12:34.140
these are the people
that are constantly

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:35.470
ratcheting up their dose.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:38.190
These are the people that
tell you no, I'm not addicted,

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:39.890
but if you were to
say, hey, all right,

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:42.390
well then let's do an experiment
where you don't take kratom

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:45.090
for a week, those are going to
be the people that are suddenly

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:47.443
going to get anxious about
the mere idea of that.

00:12:47.443 --> 00:12:49.110
I've also talked about
this in reference

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:51.990
to cannabis when I did an
episode about cannabis.

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:53.940
Again I'm not putting
a blanket statement out

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:55.813
there about cannabis
as being good or bad.

00:12:55.813 --> 00:12:57.480
On that episode, I
talked about the fact

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:01.030
that cannabis does indeed
have various therapeutic uses.

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:03.300
But that there are a
good number of people

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:05.580
who rely on cannabis
for anxiety control

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:09.750
and other ways of modulating
their brain chemistry

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:11.873
and bodily chemistry such
that if you were to say,

00:13:11.873 --> 00:13:13.290
hey, are you
addicted to cannabis?

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:15.060
They'd say no, I don't
have to smoke cannabis.

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:16.440
I don't have to take my edibles.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:18.730
But if you were to push
them a little bit and say,

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:21.060
all right, well then,
let's do an experiment

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:23.670
where you don't
consume any cannabis

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:25.870
in any form for 10 days.

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:28.530
They don't like the idea
of that experiment at all.

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:30.960
So much so that were they
to run that experiment,

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:33.210
they would experience a lot
of the withdrawal symptoms

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:35.830
associated with addiction.

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:37.770
So I can't, in good
faith, say that kratom

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:40.830
is safe for everybody
because it is simply not.

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:43.690
Now is it safer than
the other opioids?

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:44.568
And if so, why?

00:13:44.568 --> 00:13:46.110
Well, in order to
address that, let's

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:49.320
take a short glance at
the pharmacology of kratom

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:52.120
and how it works in terms of
its neurochemistry in the body.

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:53.250
Now I'm going to keep
this pretty brief

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:54.625
because, in the
future, I'm going

00:13:54.625 --> 00:13:57.060
to do an extended episode
all about opioids,

00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:59.310
and I will include kratom
in that conversation.

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:04.590
But just to back up a little bit
and discuss what opioids are.

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:07.200
Opioids are compounds
that can activate

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:09.470
the so-called endogenous
opioid system.

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:11.840
All of us have within
our brain and body

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:15.380
the capacity to release
our own opioids.

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:16.010
That's right.

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:17.970
You have opioids
within your body.

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.120
They are released from
neurons, and they bind

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:22.457
to so-called opioid receptors.

00:14:22.457 --> 00:14:25.040
Perhaps some of you have heard
of the so-called runner's high.

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:27.950
The runner's high
is a euphoric state.

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:30.390
It's a fairly mild euphoric
state in most cases.

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:32.060
But it's a euphoric
state induced

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:35.600
by long-duration effort, a.k.a.

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:36.680
the runner's high.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:38.630
The runner's high
is just a phrase

00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:40.370
used to explain
that when we engage

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:44.180
in long-duration
repetitive action effort,

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:47.630
the body starts releasing these
endogenous opioids they bind

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:49.190
to receptors, and
they trigger things

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:51.680
like analgesia,
relief from pain.

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:53.383
They trigger mild euphoria.

00:14:53.383 --> 00:14:54.800
They tend to change
our perception

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:56.175
of the outside
world, make things

00:14:56.175 --> 00:14:57.950
look more beautiful, shiny.

00:14:57.950 --> 00:14:59.780
They give things a
little bit of a sheen.

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:02.582
What I just described
is a mild version

00:15:02.582 --> 00:15:04.040
of what people
experience when they

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:05.293
take something like morphine.

00:15:05.293 --> 00:15:06.710
When people take
morphine, there's

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:08.600
a more of a sedative
effect, there's

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:10.730
more of a euphoric
effect, and there's

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:11.880
more of a dreamlike effect.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:13.430
And again, it will
depend on dosage.

00:15:13.430 --> 00:15:16.940
Kratom and morphine,
and hydrocodone all

00:15:16.940 --> 00:15:21.830
have the property of acting
like the endogenous opioids

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:24.560
but at much higher potency.

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:26.270
When you bring them
into your system,

00:15:26.270 --> 00:15:28.110
they cross the
blood-brain barrier.

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:29.930
So they go into
the brain, and they

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:33.140
bind to a number of
different opioid receptors.

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:35.540
There's the mu-opioid receptor.

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:38.750
By the way, these names all
follow Greek alphabet letters.

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:41.810
So the mu-opioid receptor,
the kappa opioid receptor,

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:43.850
there are a bunch
of different ones.

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:47.960
Kratom binds preferentially
to the mu-opioid receptor

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:50.127
and somewhat to the
kappa opioid receptor.

00:15:50.127 --> 00:15:51.710
There's a big
misconception out there.

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:53.127
A lot of people,
especially people

00:15:53.127 --> 00:15:54.920
who are proponents
of kratom, will

00:15:54.920 --> 00:15:59.570
say no morphine and hydrocodone
bind the mu-opioid receptor,

00:15:59.570 --> 00:16:02.040
whereas kratom binds the
kappa opioid receptor.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:03.530
So it's a different compound.

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:04.700
Very, very different.

00:16:04.700 --> 00:16:05.930
Not true.

00:16:05.930 --> 00:16:07.560
Not true.

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:11.040
Kratom, morphine, and
hydrocodone all bind

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:12.690
to the mu-opioid receptor.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:14.910
And that's what's
largely responsible

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:17.740
for its opioid-like effects.

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:20.640
Especially when you get
the dosage up to a level

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:23.250
where you start getting the
mild sedation, the analgesia,

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:23.970
the pain relief.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:26.520
And keep in mind a lot of
people are taking kratom

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:28.530
because they want pain relief.

00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:31.620
And when that mu-opioid
receptor system is activated,

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:35.910
it indirectly activates a lot
of the reinforcement circuitry

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:38.620
in the brain that relates
to dopamine and serotonin.

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:40.710
This is another area that
we'll go into in more

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:41.370
depth in the future.

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:42.953
But there's been a
lot of controversy.

00:16:42.953 --> 00:16:44.580
People saying, oh,
you know kratom

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:46.150
doesn't trigger the
dopamine system,

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:47.460
therefore, it's not addictive.

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:48.627
But that is simply not true.

00:16:48.627 --> 00:16:50.790
It indirectly activates
the reinforcement circuitry

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:52.980
that includes both
dopamine and serotonin.

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:56.070
But its primary effect is to
hit this mu-opioid receptor

00:16:56.070 --> 00:16:58.820
system that exists in a
bunch of places in the brain

00:16:58.820 --> 00:17:00.570
but mainly in the
brainstem in a structure

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:02.550
called the periaqueductal
gray nucleus, which

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:04.720
then provides pain relief.

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:07.829
It triggers a number of
different shifts in cognition.

00:17:07.829 --> 00:17:11.230
It's what creates that mild
euphoria, et cetera, et cetera.

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:15.180
Now how can we be so confident
that kratom is acting so

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:18.300
similarly to hydrocodone
and to morphine,

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:20.400
albeit with lower potency?

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:22.109
And the reason is
if you give people

00:17:22.109 --> 00:17:25.170
a drug that blocks the
mu-opioid receptor prior to them

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:27.030
taking kratom, they
don't experience

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:30.330
any of the classic
effects of taking kratom.

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:34.860
No euphoria, no
analgesia, no sedation.

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:37.600
Even at the low dosages
no mild stimulant effect.

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:41.700
So we really have to look
at kratom as an opioid, OK.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:43.060
That's just the honest truth.

00:17:43.060 --> 00:17:45.227
And if you're somebody who
doesn't like this message

00:17:45.227 --> 00:17:47.277
because you like kratom,
I'm not telling you

00:17:47.277 --> 00:17:49.860
that you don't like kratom, I'm
telling you likely like kratom

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:51.400
because it's an opioid.

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:53.850
So again, if you go online
and you start asking questions

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:56.430
about kratom, again, you're
going to see these two camps.

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:59.370
You're going to see
the kratom is terrible,

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:01.950
it's addictive, it's
everything bad camp.

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:05.130
And then you're going to see the
other camp out there very vocal

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:08.550
as well, talking about how
kratom helped them avoid

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:09.720
other things that are worse.

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:12.900
And this is an argument
that, frankly, I

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:14.700
don't think we want
to lean on, right.

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:18.810
The idea that substance A is
not as bad as substance B.

00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:21.540
And that making
substance A unavailable

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:26.040
is just going to send everyone
running for substance B. That's

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:27.810
a weak argument, frankly.

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:29.430
I heard this argument
around cannabis.

00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:32.092
And by the way, I think pretty
balanced about cannabis.

00:18:32.092 --> 00:18:34.050
If you listen to the
episode I did on cannabis,

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:36.960
I think you'll agree that
I believe that cannabis has

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:38.560
its therapeutic applications.

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:42.450
I also believe that young
people, especially young males

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:44.460
with a predisposition
to psychosis,

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:46.860
should not be taking
high-concentration THC

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:47.580
cannabis.

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:50.160
Because the data tell
us they are already

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:52.650
at risk of psychosis, and they
are at a much greater risk

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:53.950
of psychosis if they do.

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:57.270
So I think I'm pretty balanced
about the story on cannabis.

00:18:57.270 --> 00:18:59.700
But I often hear the
argument oh cannabis

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:03.690
isn't as bad as alcohol and,
therefore, cannabis is good.

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:05.330
That's simply not rational.

00:19:05.330 --> 00:19:07.080
What we should be
saying is whether or not

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:09.030
it's cannabis or
alcohol or kratom

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:11.550
or any substance for
that matter, what

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:14.700
are the potential benefits,
what are the potential risks?

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:17.010
And again, this is
far too much than we

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:18.780
can go into in this AMA.

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:21.390
And we will go into in a
future full-length episode

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:22.740
of the Huberman Lab podcast.

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:25.020
But the other thing
to really keep in mind

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:29.100
is the lack of regulation
over kratom supplements.

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:31.410
Now as many of you
know, I am a fan

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:33.810
of many, not all,
but many supplements

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:35.050
in certain conditions.

00:19:35.050 --> 00:19:37.890
I always say behavior is
first, then nutrition.

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:41.910
Then if and only if it's
needed, rely on supplementation.

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:44.400
But supplementation
is a big category.

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:46.110
But when we're talking
about supplements

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:48.120
that are opioid
compounds, I start

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:50.460
to get nervous because
of the high abuse

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:54.930
potential and the high
addiction potential of opioids.

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:57.150
And kratom is included
in that category.

00:19:57.150 --> 00:19:59.520
A couple of other key
notes about kratom.

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:03.030
While death directly from
kratom is fairly rare,

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:04.500
it has happened.

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:08.780
Now hydrocodone and morphine
suppress respiration.

00:20:08.780 --> 00:20:11.180
Actually, suppress breathing
by way of a mechanism

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:13.790
that, if you're a listener
of the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:20:13.790 --> 00:20:15.030
you know about.

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:16.930
Which is the so-called
physiological sigh.

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:19.430
Many of you have heard me talk
about the physiological sigh.

00:20:19.430 --> 00:20:21.680
Is something that you do
voluntarily the double Inhale

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:23.420
through the nose and
then a long exhale

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:26.060
in order to rapidly reduce
your level of stress.

00:20:26.060 --> 00:20:27.920
As far as we know,
that's the fastest way

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:30.810
to deliberately lower
your level of stress.

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:33.980
But the physiological sigh
was discovered in the 1930s

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:36.350
as a spontaneous
pattern of breathing

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:40.190
that occurs involuntarily
in sleep in order

00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:42.440
to reinflate the lungs
and offload carbon dioxide

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:44.210
and bring oxygen
back into the system

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:45.440
on the subsequent breath.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:47.570
When I interviewed
Dr. Jack Feldman, who

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:50.180
is a professor at University
of California Los Angeles

00:20:50.180 --> 00:20:53.030
and really the pioneer of
the modern neuroscience

00:20:53.030 --> 00:20:56.030
understanding of respiration,
he talked about some studies

00:20:56.030 --> 00:20:58.100
in his laboratory that
were exploring why

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:01.230
people die when taking opioids.

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:03.800
This is a major issue associated
with the opioid crisis.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:06.690
And what he mentioned
was that the opioids bind

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:08.490
to particular
receptors in the brain

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:12.810
stem locations that generate
physiological sighs.

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:18.240
So opioids, when taken as drugs,
suppress physiological sighing

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:19.860
and do so during sleep.

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:22.290
And lack of adequate
respiration,

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:24.540
meaning people stop
breathing during sleep,

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:26.340
is one of the primary
reasons why people

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:28.870
die when they take opioids.

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:31.410
Now kratom is not known
to suppress respiration

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:33.630
when taken at low
to moderate dosages.

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:36.660
But when combined with
any other opioids,

00:21:36.660 --> 00:21:39.060
and certainly when
combined with alcohol,

00:21:39.060 --> 00:21:41.010
it can suppress respiration.

00:21:41.010 --> 00:21:43.770
And while the data on
this are fairly scant,

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:46.380
there is some evidence
that kratom-induced death

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:49.840
is caused by suppression
of the respiration system.

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:52.830
So again, death due
to taking kratom

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:54.840
at the dosages that
are recommended

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:57.780
on most commercial
packaging is fairly rare,

00:21:57.780 --> 00:21:59.520
although it has happened.

00:21:59.520 --> 00:22:01.230
It's unclear if it
happened because it

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:03.360
was taken in combination
with other compounds.

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:04.183
That seems likely.

00:22:04.183 --> 00:22:05.850
But we can't forget
that a lot of people

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:08.850
are taking kratom at much
higher dosages and, in fact,

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:11.160
progressively higher
and higher dosages

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:13.470
from these
over-the-counter sources.

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:18.180
And with increased dosage,
there is, yes, an increased risk

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:19.600
of respiratory failure.

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:22.200
So again, all of this
points to the fact

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:24.820
that kratom is simply
not a benign substance.

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:26.850
So if we're going
to be very honest,

00:22:26.850 --> 00:22:29.530
the addictive potential
of kratom is real.

00:22:29.530 --> 00:22:33.390
It is exacerbated for some
people compared to others,

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:34.800
but it is real.

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:38.430
It's very similarities to other
more potent forms of opioids

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:40.680
are what make it attractive
for some people who

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:43.470
are trying to come off those
more potent form of opioids.

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:46.590
But the goal, of course,
is to completely come off

00:22:46.590 --> 00:22:47.760
all opioids.

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:50.640
And kratom itself can
be a bit of a trap.

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:52.860
It can be a trap in
the sense that people

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:54.750
who have never
taken other opioids

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:56.700
can become addicted
to kratom itself.

00:22:56.700 --> 00:22:58.980
That is absolutely
clear that can happen.

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:01.780
It has happened in a
great number of people.

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:04.350
It's also clear that kratom
can potentially be a trap.

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:05.700
Notice I said potentially.

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:08.100
Because if people are
trying to come off

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:09.690
other more potent
forms of opioids

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:11.340
and then they use
kratom to do that,

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:14.670
and then they're ratcheting
up their dosage of kratom such

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:18.930
that they're now matching the
endogenous response to kratom

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:22.440
in a way that doesn't really
distinguish from the effects

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:24.900
that they were getting from
morphine and hydrocodone,

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:28.290
well then they're just
using a different form

00:23:28.290 --> 00:23:29.940
of morphine and hydrocodone.

00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:31.920
And I'm sure that some
of you are out there

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:33.480
saying that is ridiculous.

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:36.150
You cannot compare the effects
of kratom to the effects

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:36.870
of hydrocodone.

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:39.803
But the potency is about
one-sixth of hydrocodone.

00:23:39.803 --> 00:23:41.220
And there are
people out there who

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:46.200
are just increasing and
increasing both the dosage

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:48.600
and modifying the type
of kratom that they're

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:50.520
taking so that they're
getting the kratom that

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:53.670
has a particularly high
concentration of one

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:58.050
of the alkaloids that hits that
mu-opioid receptor hardest.

00:23:58.050 --> 00:24:00.450
And in doing so,
sure, they're not

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:02.520
getting the pure
hydrocodone effect,

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:03.960
but they're getting
really close.

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:06.630
So my advice would be, if
you haven't touched kratom,

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:08.610
don't touch it at all, ever.

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:11.010
If you are taking
kratom, you need

00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:12.715
to take note of what
we just discussed.

